    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-24s-poe",
    "note": "Tech specs print PoE availability as six rows across two PSU options. As shipped - Power Supply \"(2) Hot-swappable AC/DC 600W power modules\" - 100-240 V AC shared mode gives 1,050 W and 100-240 V AC redundant mode 450 W; 1,050 W is what this spec models. The other four rows need the 1,200 W modules the page marks \"1,200W power modules sold separately\": 200-240 V AC shared 2,250 W / 100-120 V AC shared 1,850 W / 200-240 V AC redundant 1,050 W / 100-120 V AC redundant 850 W. A lab that has fitted those modules can raise the ceiling to 2,250 W on a 200-240 V feed and must say so. Per-port ceiling is 90 W on all 24 copper ports (802.3bt Type 4, Class 8): 15.4 W PoE / 30 W PoE+ / 60 W PoE++ / 90 W PoE+++."
  },
  "notes": "Ethernet census read from the vendor's own Port Layout table: https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-24s-poe (HTTP 200, 677,049 B, server-rendered, read 2026-11-20) prints six rows - \"1 GbE RJ45 = 1 (1G/100M/10M)\" with the vendor tooltip \"OOB Management port only\", \"2.5 GbE RJ45 = 8 (All PoE+++)\", \"10 GbE RJ45 = 16 (All PoE+++)\", \"25G SFP28 = 4\", \"100G QSFP28 = 2\" (tooltip: \"Stacking port only, does not support uplink. Breakout cables are not supported\"), \"Console = 1\". Before this correction the spec carried five of those six rows - 31 of 32 jacks - so the serial console was absent and nothing could be cabled to the out-of-band path; the 1G OOB port and both RJ45 banks were also missing the 10M rung the page prints. PoE: 24 PoE+++ ports with no budget in the spec, so the loader named it from the model string, found no wattage and served 0 W while the description beside it claimed 1,050 W. Also recorded, not modelled as ports: Max. Power Consumption 150 W excluding PoE / 2,400 W including; Switching Capacity 460 Gbps; non-blocking 230 Gbps; 342 Mpps; stacking up to 4 switches of the same port count; (2) AC inputs, (4) hot-swappable fans."
}
